SINA_SMART["cellbio1-membrane"] = {"lecture":"cellbio1-membrane","title":"The plasma membrane: structure, exchanges and signalling","mapStatus":"auto: generated by universal.py from the lecture summary and key points; not human-reviewed","los":[{"id":"cellbio1-membrane:e0","text":"Asymmetric fluid mosaic; red cell membrane 40% lipid, 50% protein, 10% carbohydrate.","topic":"The big picture","sec":0,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"cellbio1-membrane:e4","text":"Carbohydrates only outside: glycocalyx.","topic":"The big picture","sec":0,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"cellbio1-membrane:e1","text":"Phospholipids (~75%): glycerol, 2 fatty acids, phosphate, choline/inositol/serine/ethanolamine.","topic":"Components","sec":1,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"cellbio1-membrane:e2","text":"Cholesterol (~25%) limits permeability.","topic":"Components","sec":1,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"cellbio1-membrane:e3","text":"Proteins: peripheral (clathrin, G proteins) vs integral/transmembrane (pumps, channels).","topic":"Components","sec":1,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"cellbio1-membrane:e5","text":"Passive: simple (gases, water) or facilitated (channels, aquaporins, GLUT4).","topic":"Exchanges","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"cellbio1-membrane:e6","text":"Active: Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase (3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in per ATP); MDR pumps → drug resistance.","topic":"Exchanges","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"cellbio1-membrane:e7","text":"Endocytosis (clathrin): phagocytosis, pinocytosis → lysosome. Exocytosis (SNARE).","topic":"Exchanges","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"cellbio1-membrane:e8","text":"Signal → receptor → kinase cascade → response; no survival signal → apoptosis.","topic":"Cell signalling","sec":3,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"cellbio1-membrane:e9","text":"GPCR → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA; GPCR → PLC → IP3 + DAG → Ca²⁺ + PKC; RTK → MAP kinase.","topic":"Cell signalling","sec":3,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"}],"vmap":{},"mlevel":{"1wflm60":"a","o9pdkk":"a","1tmpo9s":"a","1awt86i":"a","h18a11":"a","13rcoi":"a","1ilwxep":"a","1i33tdw":"a","lebfhr":"a","pknfrk":"a","1shtqdb":"a","orxo6o":"a","rranqk":"a","1cgwcmg":"a","1kaonmy":"a","4piuzm":"x"},"mapCounts":{"verified":0,"auto":15,"ambiguous":0,"missing":1},"mrules":[],"items":[],"experimental":[],"status":{},"difficulty":{},"expl":{"cellbio1-membrane:e0":{"s":0,"u":["Asymmetric fluid mosaic; red cell membrane 40% lipid, 50% protein, 10% carbohydrate.","The red cell membrane is about 40% lipids, 50% proteins, 10% carbohydrates .","The plasma membrane is an asymmetric fluid mosaic : lipids, proteins and carbohydrates (the mosaic) that move in all directions (fluid), with sugars only on the outer face (asymmetric)."],"dp":[]},"cellbio1-membrane:e4":{"s":0,"u":["Carbohydrates only outside: glycocalyx.","The red cell membrane is about 40% lipids, 50% proteins, 10% carbohydrates ."],"dp":[]},"cellbio1-membrane:e1":{"s":1,"u":["Phospholipids (~75%): glycerol, 2 fatty acids, phosphate, choline/inositol/serine/ethanolamine.","Phospholipids (~75% of lipids): glycerol + 2 hydrophobic fatty acid tails + phosphate + one small hydrophilic molecule (choline, inositol, serine or ethanolamine), forming the bilayer with heads outward."],"dp":[]},"cellbio1-membrane:e2":{"s":1,"u":["Cholesterol (~25%) limits permeability.","Cholesterol (~25%): an alcohol group (hydrophilic), 4 rings and a hydrocarbon chain; inserted between phospholipids, it limits permeability ."],"dp":[]},"cellbio1-membrane:e3":{"s":1,"u":["Proteins: peripheral (clathrin, G proteins) vs integral/transmembrane (pumps, channels).","Proteins : peripheral (hydrophilic, on either face; e.g. clathrin, G proteins) and integral (amphipathic, partly buried; transmembrane proteins such as pumps, carriers and ion channels)."],"dp":[]},"cellbio1-membrane:e5":{"s":2,"u":["Passive: simple (gases, water) or facilitated (channels, aquaporins, GLUT4).","Small molecules (amino acids, fatty acids, ions, glucose) cross without deforming it: passive transport (no ATP, down the concentration gradient) by simple diffusion (gases O₂, CO₂, NO; water; some ions; small uncharged molecules like urea, glycerol) or facilitated diffusion through aquaporins (water), ion channels and carrier proteins (e.g."],"dp":[]},"cellbio1-membrane:e6":{"s":2,"u":["Active: Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase (3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in per ATP); MDR pumps → drug resistance.","Active transport uses ATP to move ions against their gradient: the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase binds intracellular Na⁺, is phosphorylated by ATP, changes shape and releases Na⁺ outside, binds extracellular K⁺, is dephosphorylated and releases K⁺ inside (1 ATP per cycle; it moves 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in).","MDR/ABC pumps expel many drugs (ATP-dependent, non-specific), explaining resistance of tumours to chemotherapy and of Plasmodium-infected cells to antimalarials."],"dp":[{"h":"Why cells need a pump that costs energy","t":"Diffusion always moves ions toward equal concentrations, which would erase the high K⁺ inside and high Na⁺ outside that cells depend on for nerve impulses, muscle contraction and volume control. The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase keeps spending ATP to push them back, maintaining gradients that other transporters then use to move glucose and amino acids. Source: the lecture's active transport slides."}]},"cellbio1-membrane:e7":{"s":2,"u":["Endocytosis (clathrin): phagocytosis, pinocytosis → lysosome. Exocytosis (SNARE).","Endocytosis on clathrin (triskelion-shaped peripheral protein): adsorption, invagination, vesicle formation and detachment, fusion with a lysosome to form a digestive vacuole; phagocytosis of solids (macrophages, Kupffer cells, osteoclasts) and pinocytosis of liquids.","Exocytosis is the reverse: vesicles fuse with the membrane via SNARE proteins and release their content (saliva, most hormones, pancreatic zymogens )."],"dp":[]},"cellbio1-membrane:e8":{"s":3,"u":["Signal → receptor → kinase cascade → response; no survival signal → apoptosis.","General scheme: signal → membrane receptor → kinase cascade → cellular response .","Cells act on signals (mitosis, metabolism, secretion, differentiation, contraction, neurotransmission, migration); a cell deprived of survival signals undergoes apoptosis ."],"dp":[{"h":"Why second messengers amplify signals","t":"One hormone molecule binding one receptor can activate several G proteins, each enzyme then makes many cAMP molecules, and each kinase phosphorylates many targets. This cascade turns a tiny outside signal into a large response inside the cell, which is why hormones work at very low concentrations. Source: the lecture's PKA and PKC pathway slides."}]},"cellbio1-membrane:e9":{"s":3,"u":["GPCR → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA; GPCR → PLC → IP3 + DAG → Ca²⁺ + PKC; RTK → MAP kinase.","PKA pathway : hormone binds a GPCR → the trimeric G protein (activated by GTP) stimulates adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → protein kinase A → phosphorylation of targets (channels, enzymes, transcription factors).","Second messengers: cAMP (PKA) and Ca²⁺ (PKC)."],"dp":[]}},"meta":{"1wflm60":{"a":"recall","vg":"cellbio1-membrane:e0/recall/1"},"o9pdkk":{"a":"recall","vg":"cellbio1-membrane:e0/recall/2"},"1tmpo9s":{"a":"recall","vg":"cellbio1-membrane:e1/recall/1"},"1awt86i":{"a":"recall","vg":"cellbio1-membrane:e2/recall/1"},"h18a11":{"a":"recall","vg":"cellbio1-membrane:e4/recall/1"},"13rcoi":{"a":"recall","vg":"cellbio1-membrane:e5/recall/1"},"1ilwxep":{"a":"recall","vg":"cellbio1-membrane:e5/recall/2"},"1i33tdw":{"a":"recall","vg":"cellbio1-membrane:e5/recall/3"},"lebfhr":{"a":"recall","vg":"cellbio1-membrane:e6/recall/1"},"pknfrk":{"a":"relationship","vg":"cellbio1-membrane:e6/relationship/1"},"1shtqdb":{"a":"recall","vg":"cellbio1-membrane:e7/recall/1"},"orxo6o":{"a":"recall","vg":"cellbio1-membrane:e7/recall/2"},"rranqk":{"a":"recall","vg":"cellbio1-membrane:e7/recall/3"},"1cgwcmg":{"a":"recall","vg":"cellbio1-membrane:e9/recall/1"},"1kaonmy":{"a":"recall","vg":"cellbio1-membrane:e8/recall/1"}},"explRev":"36289b15c1cd"};
